# Material 3 experimental opt-in — per-function only

## What the warning is

When you call a `P*` design-system primitive (or a Material 3 component directly), Kotlin may emit:

```
This material API is experimental and is likely to change or to be removed in the future.
```

It's compile-time only — nothing breaks at runtime. The cause: the component (or one of its default parameter values, or a type in its public signature) is annotated with `@ExperimentalMaterial3Api`, and Kotlin propagates that requirement to every caller.

## Why it leaks from `P*` primitives

Several of our `P*` wrappers internally `@OptIn(ExperimentalMaterial3Api::class)` themselves but **still leak the requirement to consumers** because their public signatures expose experimental types or default expressions:

- **`PModalBottomSheet`** — default value `rememberModalBottomSheetState(...)` and `SheetState` parameter type are both experimental.
- **`PTopBar`** — wraps `TopAppBar` / `CenterAlignedTopAppBar`, both experimental.
- **`PScaffold`** — when its `topBar` slot embeds `PTopBar`, the experimental requirement propagates upward through the call.
- Any future `P*` wrapping `SearchBar`, `PullToRefreshBox`, `SegmentedButton`, etc.

This is a known Kotlin/Compose limitation. We **don't** "fix" it at the primitive (doing so would require either marking the `P*` itself experimental — same problem — or hiding the experimental types behind non-experimental wrappers, which would mean re-implementing state management). The pragmatic, codebase-wide convention is to opt in at the call site.

## The convention — apply this every time

**Per-function annotation directly above `@Composable`** + the matching import:

```kotlin
import androidx.compose.material3.ExperimentalMaterial3Api

@OptIn(ExperimentalMaterial3Api::class)
@Composable
fun MyScreen(...) {
    PScaffold(topBar = { PTopBar(...) }) { ... }
}
```

Apply to whatever composable function the warning points to (the **enclosing** composable, not the helper that contains the call). For sheet wrappers, that's typically the public entry composable; for screens with extracted top-bar helpers like `private fun DetailTopBar(...)`, annotate the helper.

### What NOT to do

| ❌ Don't | Why |
|---|---|
| `@file:OptIn(ExperimentalMaterial3Api::class)` | Inconsistent with the rest of the codebase — every other file uses per-function. Reviewers also lose the per-call signal that experimental API is in play. |
| Module-wide `-opt-in=androidx.compose.material3.ExperimentalMaterial3Api` in `build.gradle.kts` | Hides future API churn from the entire module. When the component graduates from experimental, you won't notice and can't audit. |
| Suppress with `@Suppress("OPT_IN_USAGE")` | Loses intent — `@OptIn` documents *which* unstable API you accepted. |
| Mark the consumer composable itself with `@ExperimentalMaterial3Api` | Propagates the requirement further up the call chain — same problem one level higher. |

## Established pattern in this codebase

Existing examples to follow when in doubt:

- `app/src/main/java/com/efthemiosprime/pasabayan/features/bookings/ui/RequestToCarrySheet.kt`
- `app/src/main/java/com/efthemiosprime/pasabayan/features/trips/ui/EditTripSheet.kt`
- `app/src/main/java/com/efthemiosprime/pasabayan/features/payments/ui/AddPaymentMethodSheet.kt`
- `app/src/main/java/com/efthemiosprime/pasabayan/features/payments/ui/PaymentMethodsScreen.kt`

All use `@OptIn(ExperimentalMaterial3Api::class)` immediately above `@Composable`, with the import alphabetically placed alongside the other `androidx.compose.material3.*` imports.

## When a component graduates from experimental

When a Compose Material 3 release (check `androidx.compose.material3.*` annotations) drops `@ExperimentalMaterial3Api` from a component we use:

1. Remove the unused `@OptIn(ExperimentalMaterial3Api::class)` annotations from consumers (Lint will flag them as unused).
2. Remove the `import androidx.compose.material3.ExperimentalMaterial3Api` from those files.
3. If **no** experimental APIs remain in use, also clean up the `@OptIn` on the corresponding `P*` primitive in `:core:designsystem`.
4. Document the change in `android-spec/14-design-system.md` under "Material 3 experimental status."

Search command to find all opt-ins:

```bash
grep -rln "ExperimentalMaterial3Api" app/src/main core/designsystem/src/main
```

